{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /work/answer.md /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prudent modal agents — answer\n\nFORMULA: B(a)|B(~a)->B(F)\nDEPTH: 1\n\n(Parsed as (□a ∨ □¬a) → □⊥. Equivalent, slightly longer: `B(F) | ~B(a) & ~B(~a)`, i.e. □a ↔ □¬a.)\n\ncheck.py: `depth 1; vs CooperateBot D, vs DefectBot D, vs FairBot C, vs itself C; prudent: YES`\n\n## 1. Outcome table (world-by-world)\n\nLet X = (□a ∨ □¬a) → □⊥. At world 0 every box is true, so X = C at world 0 against everyone. For n ≥ 1, □⊥ is\nfalse, so X cooperates at n iff neither □a nor □¬a holds, i.e. iff the opponent's `a` took **both** values at worlds < n.\n\n| opponent | X's moves at worlds 0,1,2,3,… | stable |\n|---|---|---|\n| CooperateBot (a ≡ ⊤) | C, D, D, D, … | **D** |\n| DefectBot (a ≡ ⊥) | C, D, D, D, … | **D** |\n| FairBot (a_n = ∧_{m<n} X_m) | a: 1,1,0,0,…; X: C, D, D, C, C, … | **C** |\n| itself (a_n = X_n) | C, D, C, C, … | **C** |\n\n## 2. Minimality: no depth-0 agent is prudent\n\nA depth-0 formula has no boxes; since it must be fully modalized it contains no atoms, so it is a Boolean\ncombination of ⊤, ⊥, i.e. equivalent to the constant ⊤ or ⊥. If ⊤, it cooperates with CooperateBot; if ⊥, it\ndefects against FairBot (and itself). Either way it is not prudent. Hence the minimal depth is exactly 1, attained\nabove. (The problem's \"evidence\" — no prudent agent among depth ≤ 2, ≤ 7 symbols — is consistent: the smallest\nprudent formula has 9 symbols, see §3.)\n\n## 3. Characterization of the depth-1 prudent agents, and minimal size\n\n**Reduction to a set function.** A depth-1 fully modalized formula is a Boolean combination of ⊤, ⊥ and boxes □ψ\nwith ψ propositional in a, c, d. At world n, □ψ holds iff every triple (a,c,d) realised at worlds m < n satisfies ψ.\nSo X's move at world n is f(S_n), where S_n ⊆ {0,1}^3 is the set of triples (a_m, c_m, d_m) seen at worlds m < n\n(S_0 = ∅), and f: P({0,1}^3) → {0,1} is determined by φ. Conversely every such f arises: S is determined by the\ntruth values of the boxes □¬χ_t (t ∉ S), χ_t the conjunction describing triple t, so f is a Boolean combination of them.\n\nWrite u = 111, v = 000, w = 100, h = 110, k = 010 (order a,c,d), and x0 = f(∅) (X's move at world 0, the same against everyone).\n\n- **vs CooperateBot**: CB cooperates with everyone, so the triple is u at every world; S_n = {u} for n ≥ 1.\n  Stable move f({u}). Need **f({u}) = 0**.\n- **vs DefectBot**: triple v always; stable move f({v}). Need **f({v}) = 0**.\n- **vs FairBot**: FB vs CB = □⊤ = 1 always; FB vs DB = □⊥ = 1 at world 0 only; FB vs X at n = ∧_{m<n} X_m.\n  World 0 triple: u. X at world 1 = f({u}) = 0, so FB's `a` is 0 from world 2 on. World 1 triple (x0,1,0); worlds ≥ 2: k.\n  Stable move f({u,(x0,1,0),k}).\n- **vs itself**: X vs CB and X vs DB are x0 at world 0 and 0 afterwards (by the two conditions above). \n  - If x0 = 0: world-0 triple v, X_1 = f({v}) = 0, triple stays v forever, stable move 0 — not prudent. So **x0 = f(∅) = 1**.\n  - With x0 = 1: triple u at world 0, X_1 = f({u}) = 0, triple v at world 1. X_2 = f({u,v}). If it is 0 the triple stays v\n    and S stays {u,v}: stable 0. So **f({u,v}) = 1**; then the world-2 triple is w, and from world 3 on S = {u,v,w}\n    whatever happens (the triple is w or v, both already seen), so the stable move is f({u,v,w}): need **f({u,v,w}) = 1**.\n  - With x0 = 1 the FairBot condition reads **f({u,h,k}) = 1**.\n\n**Theorem.** A depth-1 agent is prudent iff its set function f satisfies\nf(∅)=1, f({111})=0, f({000})=0, f({111,000})=1, f({111,000,100})=1, f({111,110,010})=1.\n(Necessity and sufficiency both follow from the case analysis above, which computes every relevant game exactly.)\nThe other 256 − 6 = 250 values of f are unconstrained, so there are exactly **2^250** prudent depth-1 agents up to\nequivalence on finite linear GL frames. Example check for our formula f(S)= [S=∅] ∨ [S contains triples with a=1 and with a=0]:\nvalues 1,0,0,1,1,1 as required. Note the pair f({u})=0, f({v})=0, f({u,v})=1 is non-monotone in S, which is why no\nformula of the form \"□(…)\" or small positive combination works, and why both □a and □¬a (or an equivalent pair) are needed.\n\n**Minimal size.** Counting symbols (atoms, T, F, ~, &, |, ->, B; parentheses not counted), an exhaustive search over\ndepth-1 formulas using this characterization (`/work/opus/minsize.py`: computes for each relevant behaviour the\nsmallest formula, by size, over all propositional inner formulas and all Boolean outer combinations, sizes ≤ 13)\nfinds the minimum is **9 symbols**, attained by `B(a)|B(~a)->B(F)`. Any prudent agent of depth ≥ 2 that is smaller would\nhave to beat 9 symbols too; the problem's evidence already excludes depth ≤ 2 with ≤ 7 symbols (we did not\nexhaustively check size 8 at depth 2, so \"9 is minimal over all depths\" is claimed only for depth 1).\n\nAlso found (depth 2, classical PrudentBot style): `B(a) & B(B(F) | ~d)` is prudent.\n\nCredits: found independently by sol and opus; characterization cross-checked on the board.\n2026-10-22 21:19 UTC [sol] Reading semantics; taking independent depth-1/2 impossibility proof and structural characterization. Will coordinate before touching shared answer.md.\n2026-10-22 21:21 UTC [sol] Found checker-prudent depth 2: B(a)&B(B(F)|~d). But depth-1 arbitrary Boolean combinations can encode sets of previously observed (a,c,d) triples; investigating possible larger depth-1 counterexample to supplied small-size evidence. Do not assume depth 2 minimal.\n2026-10-22 21:22 UTC [sol] BREAKTHROUGH: depth-1 prudent formula B(F)|(~B(a)&~B(~a)), confirmed check.py. Initial C via B(F); then D until opponent a has taken both truth values. CB/DB are constant so defect; self alternation starts C,D,C,C,...; FairBot induces C,D,D,C,C,... . Depth 0 formulas are constants, so minimum depth is 1. I propose this as final formula; preparing full proof and optional characterization in /work/sol_notes.md. Please review and coordinate answer.md ownership via board.\n[opus 2026-10-22] Found DEPTH 1 prudent agent: B(a) <-> B(~a), i.e. B(F) | (~B(a) & ~B(~a)). check.py says prudent. Depth 0 impossible (constant). Writing answer.md now with characterization of depth-1 prudent agents.\n2026-10-22 21:24 UTC [sol] Agree opus owns answer.md and can submit after review. Independently derived exact depth-1 characterization: let f(S) be output as function of set S of prior triples (a,c,d); u=111,v=000,w=100,h=110,k=010. Prudence iff f(empty)=1, f({u})=f({v})=0, and f({u,v})=f({u,v,w})=f({u,h,k})=1. Other 250 subset-values arbitrary, hence 2^250 equivalence classes (all f representable with membership tests ~B(~chi_t)). Proof: initial 0 plus f({000})=0 traps self at 0; initial1 gives self history u,v then w iff f({u,v})=1. FB history always u,h,k,k,... since X-FB at1=f({u})=0. Will independently review answer when available.\n2026-10-22 21:26 UTC [sol] Independently validated six-condition characterization against 10,000 random 256-bit truth tables using direct set-history simulation (/work/sol_characterization_check.py), all agree. Counting 2^250 classes is sound because each subset is realizable as predecessor triple-set and each membership predicate is depth 1. Candidate checker again reports YES, depth 1. Awaiting opus answer for final review.\n[opus 21:20] answer.md written (formula B(a)|B(~a)->B(F), 9 symbols = min at depth 1 by exhaustive search /work/opus/minsize.py; depth-0 proof; 6-condition characterization, 2^250 classes). sol/argon: please review; will write SUBMIT after a review or in ~5 min.\n2026-10-22 21:27 UTC [sol] Optional smaller syntax: (B(a)|B(~a))->B(F) is equivalent to candidate, checker-prudent depth 1, only 9 AST symbols if implication counts one; ~ (B(a)|B(~a)) | B(F) is 10. No need to change readable chosen formula; characterization fully answers optional Q3.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
